knowledge:
- key_parameters:
    - which.proteinid=Specifies which column to use for protein names in converters (e.g., "Protein.Accessions" or "Master.Protein.Accessions" for Proteome Discoverer; "Proteins", "Leading.proteins", or "Leading.razor.proteins" for MaxQuant). ;;
    - useUniquePeptide=Logical (default TRUE) indicating whether to remove peptides that are assigned to more than one protein. ;;
    - rmPSM_withfewMea_withinRun=Logical (default TRUE) indicating whether to remove features that have only 1 or 2 measurements within each Run. ;;
    - method=The protein summarization method to use: "msstats" (default, which performs missing value imputation), "MedianPolish", "Median", or "LogSum". ;;
    - global_norm=Logical (default TRUE) indicating whether to perform global median normalization on peptide-level data before summarization. ;;
    - reference_norm=Logical (default TRUE) indicating whether to perform reference channel-based normalization between MS runs. Requires at least one reference channel annotated as "Norm" in the Condition column. ;;
    - contrast.matrix=A matrix specifying the comparisons of interest between conditions, or "pairwise" (default) to compare all possible pairs. ;;
    - moderated=Logical (default TRUE) indicating whether to calculate moderated t-statistics (TRUE) or ordinary t-statistics (FALSE).
- alternatives:
    - MSstats=An R package preferred for label-free or labeled synthetic peptide experiments (LC-MS, SRM, DIA/SWATH), whereas MSstatsTMT is specifically tailored for TMT labeling experiments. ;;
    - proteinSummarization methods=The "msstats" method imputes missing values using an Accelerated Failure Time model, whereas "MedianPolish", "Median", and "LogSum" do not perform imputation.
- when_to_use: Use this package when analyzing shotgun mass spectrometry-based proteomic experiments with tandem mass tag (TMT) labeling to detect differentially abundant proteins across multiple conditions.
- pitfalls:
    - Annotation files are not automatically generated by peptide quantification tools (like Proteome Discoverer or MaxQuant); users must manually construct an annotation data frame containing columns: Run, Fraction, TechRepMixture, Channel, Condition, BioReplicate, and Mixture. ;;
    - Reference normalization (reference_norm = TRUE) requires at least one reference channel in each MS run, annotated by "Norm" in the Condition column. If the dataset contains only one run, reference_norm must be set to FALSE. ;;
    - Shared peptides are removed by default (useUniquePeptide = TRUE), which may significantly reduce the number of features if the database contains highly homologous proteins.
